    trackers: ["security"],
    category: "漏洞與威脅情報",
    title: "惡意VS Code主題與WhatsApp釣魚攻擊分析：揭露多重資安威脅與後量子密碼缺口",
    summary: "本週資安威脅報告涵蓋多種複雜的攻擊手法。首先，研究機構Socket發現了兩個可疑的VS Code主題（Coca-Cola Christmas 和 Aurora Borealis Studio Theme），它們與先前已移除的惡意擴充功能 Aurora Nocturne Night Theme 有關聯。進一步分析揭示，這些主題包含的載入器可解密並執行嵌入式 JavaScript，並使用 Solana 交易備忘錄作為死訊投放器，顯示攻擊者持續利用開源生態系統進行惡意植入。\n\n在惡意軟體方面，一個透過 WhatsApp 傳遞的金融文件釣魚誘餌（Statement.exe）被發現，它能部署一個名為 VulcanRAT207 的 WebSocket 遠端存取木馬（RAT）。該木馬在多階段入侵中，能執行系統偵測、提升權限、注入下載器，甚至使用簽名驅動程式（GoFly64.sys）終止安全程序，具備高度的系統控制能力。\n\n此外，報告警示醫療設備面臨後量子密碼（PQC）的重大缺口。Forescout 分析指出，大多數醫療設備無法升級至 PQC，使得敏感的醫療數據容易遭受未來量子計算攻擊的「現在收割、稍後解密」（HNDL）威脅。這凸顯了醫療產業在資安韌性上的巨大挑戰。",
    tags: ["VS Code", "Malicious Extension", "WhatsApp", "VulcanRAT207", "PQC", "IoMT", "Ransomware"],
    title_en: "Malicious VS Code Themes and WhatsApp Phishing Attack Analysis: Unveiling Multiple Cybersecurity Threats and Post-Quantum Cryptography Gaps",
    summary_en: "This week's cybersecurity threat report covers several complex attack methodologies. First, the research organization Socket discovered two suspicious VS Code themes (Coca-Cola Christmas and Aurora Borealis Studio Theme) that are related to the previously removed malicious extension, Aurora Nocturne Night Theme. Further analysis revealed that the loaders contained within these themes can decrypt and execute embedded JavaScript, utilizing Solana transaction memos as dead-drop dispensers, indicating that attackers continue to exploit the open-source ecosystem for malicious implants.\n\nRegarding malware, a financial document phishing lure (Statement.exe) transmitted via WhatsApp was discovered. This lure can deploy a WebSocket Remote Access Trojan (RAT) named VulcanRAT207. During a multi-stage intrusion, this RAT can perform system detection, privilege escalation, inject downloaders, and even use a signed driver (GoFly64.sys) to terminate security processes, demonstrating highly advanced system control capabilities.\n\nFurthermore, the report warns that medical devices face significant gaps concerning Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC). Forescout's analysis indicates that most medical devices cannot be upgraded to PQC, making sensitive medical data vulnerable to",
